<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.1" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="42349-1" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Reason for referral (narrative)"/>
    <title>Reason for Referral</title>
    <text><paragraph>Severe opioid use disorder and moderate alcohol use disorder in a 33-year-old singer, five weeks after release from about ten months in custody, when her tolerance is low and her overdose risk high. Buprenorphine with naloxone was started today. Please confirm the medication plan and dose, advise on a medicine for her drinking that is safe with buprenorphine, and help set the monitoring and recovery plan. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Urgency: Within one week, on the same day as her one-week primary care visit. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Questions for the consultant:</paragraph><list listType="ordered"><item>Is buprenorphine the right medication for her, and what dose should we aim for?</item><item>Which medicine for alcohol use disorder can she take with buprenorphine?</item><item>How should we read her liver tests, and does she need more than FIB-4 to stage fibrosis?</item><item>What monitoring do you advise, including urine drug testing and visit frequency?</item><item>How should we respond if she returns to opioid use?</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Ms. Holiday, a 33-year-old singer, is seen at the request of Dr. Mayfield one week after starting buprenorphine with naloxone. She has taken every dose, 4 mg on the first day and 8 mg since, and has used no opioids. She has had no withdrawal, but cravings return each evening, especially after shows. She drinks four or five drinks on performance nights to calm her nerves. She has no history of withdrawal seizures or delirium. She sought treatment for heroin use years ago and says she wants to stay off it and keep singing. Her biggest fear is overdosing now that her tolerance is low. She tours often and worries about getting prescriptions filled on the road. She asked that this note be shared only with Dr. Mayfield. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>Billie Holiday was born Eleanora Fagan in Philadelphia on April 7, 1915, and was raised in Baltimore by her mother, Sarah Julia &quot;Sadie&quot; Fagan. [D]</item><item>Her mother died in October 1945; afterwards Holiday drank more heavily and her drug use increased as she grieved. [D]</item><item>Holiday began using heroin in the early 1940s, and her addiction became severe in the late 1940s; she was arrested repeatedly over her heroin use in that period. [D]</item><item>In May 1947 Holiday was arrested for narcotics possession; she pleaded guilty, was sentenced to a year and a day, and was held at the federal facility for women at Alderson, West Virginia, until her early release on March 16, 1948. [D]</item><item>Holiday sought treatment for her addiction, at one point checking herself into an institution in the hope of breaking her habit. [D]</item><item>Her conviction cost her the New York City cabaret card she needed to perform in clubs that served alcohol. [D]</item><item>The Federal Bureau of Narcotics pursued Holiday for years, and later writers have described that pursuit as driven by racism and linked it to her performance of &quot;Strange Fruit,&quot; a song against lynching. [R]</item></list><paragraph>3 later historical claims are omitted because they describe events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

