<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.8"/>
    <code code="51848-0" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation note"/>
    <title>Assessment</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item>Opioid use disorder, severe, early in buprenorphine treatment (F11.20). Buprenorphine is a good choice: it lowers the risk of overdose death, can be prescribed from primary care, and fits her touring better than daily methadone clinic attendance, which remains an option if buprenorphine does not control her cravings. Extended-release naltrexone would need a long opioid-free period and does not protect her if she loses tolerance and uses again. Starting at a low dose was right after enforced abstinence. Evening cravings at 8 mg mean the dose is too low; most people do best at 16 mg or more. [S]</item><item>Alcohol use disorder, moderate (F10.20). Naltrexone, the usual first choice, cannot be used with buprenorphine. Acamprosate is safe with buprenorphine and with her normal kidney function; topiramate is a second choice. Her withdrawal risk is low, so she does not need a medically managed withdrawal. [S]</item><item>Liver. AST higher than ALT with a raised GGT and a high MCV points to alcohol. Albumin, bilirubin, and platelets are normal, and FIB-4 is 1.18, below 1.3, so advanced fibrosis is unlikely and elastography is not needed now. Repeating liver tests as drinking changes, and FIB-4 within a year, is enough. She is not immune to hepatitis B and should complete the series. [S]</item><item>Return to use. A return to use is common and is a reason to strengthen treatment, not to stop it. Her greatest risk is a gap in her medication while touring. [S]</item></list><paragraph>Data reviewed: Dr. Mayfield&apos;s intake note with the safety assessment, AUDIT-C, DAST-10, and PHQ-9. Last week&apos;s results: AST 52, ALT 38, GGT 84 U/L; albumin 4.0 g/dL; bilirubin 0.7 mg/dL; MCV 101 fL; platelets 236; creatinine 0.72 mg/dL; PEth 312 ng/mL; hepatitis C antibody nonreactive (0.09); hepatitis B surface antigen and core antibody nonreactive, surface antibody 3 mIU/mL; HIV antigen-antibody nonreactive. Today&apos;s urine drug test: buprenorphine and norbuprenorphine only. Prescription drug monitoring program: no other controlled-substance prescriptions. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.10" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="18776-5" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Plan of care note"/>
    <title>Plan of Treatment</title>
    <text><list><item>Increase buprenorphine with naloxone to 16 mg once daily today. [S]</item><item>Start acamprosate 666 mg three times daily; continue thiamine while she drinks heavily. [S]</item><item>Naloxone at home and in her bag, with her friend trained; fentanyl test strips; never use alone. [S]</item><item>Visits every one to two weeks for the first month, then every two to four weeks until stable, then monthly; telehealth check-ins when she is on tour. [S]</item><item>Urine drug testing with fentanyl at most visits early on, explained as part of care, never as a condition of treatment; check the prescription drug monitoring program before each prescription. [S]</item><item>PEth with liver tests at each check while she is drinking; FIB-4 again at six to twelve months; elastography only if FIB-4 reaches 1.3. [S]</item><item>Complete the hepatitis A and B vaccine series; repeat hepatitis C and HIV testing yearly while any drug use continues. [S]</item><item>If she returns to use, keep her on buprenorphine, increase support, and look for the cause; consider monthly extended-release buprenorphine if gaps in supply recur. [S]</item><item>Plan for tours before each trip: a pharmacy chain that can fill in any state, prior authorization kept current, an early fill, and a travel letter. [S]</item><item>Offer counseling and peer recovery support without making them a condition of medication. [S]</item><item>Discussed with Dr. Mayfield, who sees her the same day. The plan was explained to Ms. Holiday, who agreed with it; at her request this note is shared only with Dr. Mayfield and is held under the federal confidentiality rule for substance use disorder records. [S]</item><item>Follow-up: Addiction medicine review at three months or sooner if there is a return to use; Dr. Mayfield continues prescribing. [S]</item></list></text>

  </section>
